 The owner's decision, recorded in [ADR-0076](decisions/ADR-0076-canvas-only-connection-authoring.md): connections are authored only on the canvas, for every node kind. The node dialog has no **Connections** section, no **Remove edge** buttons and no **Connect** form. Everything else in it stays: id and label, the config form, declaration editing (decision routes, Choice options, Noul sides, Score bands, Script exit-code rows and Repeat route settings) with its consequence reviews that name connections, **Delete node**, protected Chat voice steps and held input.
 
 - **Create.** Drag from an output handle to an input handle, or click an output and then an input. A drag may start at either end. One edge per output; any number may enter one input; triggers have no input and exits no output. A drop on a port that refuses the connection shows the reason in the editor's "Connection refused: …" notice (`onConnectEnd` and the click-connect end in `editor/Canvas.tsx`, using `connectionProblem` in `editor/model.ts`); an occupied output says how to free it ("select its connection and choose Remove connection (or press Delete or Backspace), then draw it again"). A drop on the empty pane or on a port of the same type says nothing and leaves an earlier notice as it was, and a refusal never creates an edge. Protected Chat voice steps refuse as before ("Voice steps stay.").
-- **Remove.** Click a connection, or focus it and press Enter or Space, to select it. Then press Delete or Backspace with focus on the canvas, or use **Remove connection** in the selected connection's toolbar beside **Reset route**: it is 32 px and 44 px under a coarse pointer, is disabled with the voice-step message on a protected edge, and moves focus to **Undo** afterwards. One connection is removed at a time; a selected node is never removed by an edge's removal. After a mouse pan the toolbar briefly refuses: d3-zoom ends a pan that moved by adding a window-capture `click.drag` listener (d3-drag's `yesdrag`) that cancels the next click anywhere on the page, and removes it in a zero-delay task, so a Remove connection click in between was swallowed while the button looked available. A per-canvas gate (`editor/viewport-gesture.ts`) is `panning` from the canvas's first `onMove` with a `mousemove` source event, `settling` from the next mouse release, and ready two tasks later. Its single passive window-capture `mouseup` listener is added when the canvas mounts, so it sees each release before d3-zoom's per-press listener stops it; it queues a zero-delay task that queues a second, and only the second, which runs after d3's cleanup, makes it ready, and only if no mouse move came since and the canvas is still mounted. Meanwhile **Remove connection** and **Reset route** are `aria-disabled` (dimmed, keeping focus, their hit target and `nopan`) with `data-gesture` set to the state, and their handlers refuse on that same rendered state: a click, Enter or Space then does nothing and says nothing, and the first click once they show as available acts once. The pan's own click is still cancelled and the selection kept. Wheel, touch, Fit View and other programmatic moves add no blocker and never touch the gate; they also cannot strand it, since it does not wait for xyflow's `onMoveEnd`, which a later wheel or programmatic end replaces. A release that never reaches the page leaves the gate unavailable until the next mouse release there, which also ends d3's gesture. Nothing is replayed, prevented or patched; a protected voice step keeps its own disabled reason. Clicks on xyflow's zoom controls and after a node drag meet the same d3 blocker and are not gated. The screen-reader description of a focused connection says "Press Enter or Space to select the connection, then Delete or Backspace to remove it; its toolbar also has Remove connection. To connect its output elsewhere, remove the connection and draw from the output again."
+- **Remove.** Click a connection, or focus it and press Enter or Space, to select it. Then press Delete or Backspace with focus on the canvas, or use **Remove connection** in the selected connection's toolbar beside **Reset route**: it is 32 px and 44 px under a coarse pointer, is disabled with the store's reason when the store would refuse the removal, and moves focus to **Undo** afterwards. One connection is removed at a time; a selected node is never removed by an edge's removal. The store's `removeEdge` removes exactly one stored record and refuses, without changing the draft, its history, revision, save state or route ownership, a protected voice step ("Voice steps stay.") and an id more than one stored edge holds ("More than one connection uses the ID "e1"; this action cannot choose just one."), as an imported or API-written draft can (`DUPLICATE_EDGE_ID`). The shared id is checked first, so a protected holder is refused the same way in either stored order; an absent id changes nothing and says nothing. The button's disabled reason is the same string (`edgeRemovalRefusal` in `editor/store.ts`), and Delete or Backspace shows it in the "Connection refused: …" notice. A selection belongs to the connection selected: its load of the draft (the store's `generation`), its id with the stored ends of every edge holding it, and its path on the canvas. A load into the mounted editor (**Reload server draft**, a device restore, a server reconcile), even of the same id and ends, an id now held with other ends (a renamed end, a Decision route ID renamed) and a port that is gone (a Script exit-code label removed, a Decision route ID left blank) forget it while rendering, and Undo does not bring it back. Moves, route edits, label edits, config edits that keep the ports, issue refreshes and their Undo keep it. **Remove connection**, **Reset route**, a nudge, a released route drag and Delete or Backspace check the selection again when they act, since rendering can lag the store